{
  "slug": "consumption-layer-generates-itself",
  "title": "The Consumption Layer Should Generate Itself",
  "thesis": "Hand-building star schemas one business question at a time is a hidden tax: it produces a drifting galaxy of inconsistent marts, fan traps, and \"which fact table\" confusion. Because the business layer (DAB) already declares entities, keys, relationships, grain, measures and history, everything the consumption layer needs is already known \u2014 so the consumption layer (DAR) should be generated from it, not typed out. The concrete generated shape is Francesco Puppini's Unified Star Schema: a single event-based \"bridge\" (super fact) built by walking the business model graph along many-to-one edges only, which makes measures additive by construction and yields an explicit sliceability contract. Every other consumption format \u2014 data mart, OBT, classic star, feature table \u2014 is then a projection/selection of the same model rather than a separate build.",
  "relevance": "high",
  "mandates": [
    {
      "claim": "Star schemas belong in the consumption layer (DAR, Data According to Requirements: the layer shaped to what the consumer needs), not in the business layer \u2014 this post is 'the other half of that argument' about what DAR should be and where it comes from.",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "DAR is the only layer allowed to contain star/USS/OBT/mart shapes; DAB must stay free of them."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The consumption layer should generate itself from the business model rather than being built by hand, one question at a time. 'The question is not which star schema to build by hand. The question is why we are building them by hand at all.'",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "DAR must be a code-generation target driven by the DAB model; no hand-authored consumption SQL as the primary path."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The generated USS is a single table, the bridge, that connects every entity to every other entity it can legitimately reach. Puppini suggested calling it a 'super fact' rather than a bridge, to break free of what 'bridge' means in the Kimball world. It holds all the relations and all the measures in one place.",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "Generate one central bridge table holding all relations and all measures \u2014 not per-subject-area facts."
    },
    {
      "claim": "In a USS you stop sorting tables into facts and dimensions. Every entity can play both roles \u2014 an order is a thing you measure and a thing you slice by, so is a customer, so is a product.",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "The generator must not classify model entities as fact vs dimension; each entity gets both measure and slicing treatment."
    },
    {
      "claim": "No fan traps, because the bridge is built to never fan out. No 'which fact table', because there is one.",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "Fan-out safety is a build-time invariant of the generator, not a query-time discipline for analysts."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The bridge must be event-based: instead of one row per entity instance, one row per measurement event, each carrying its own date. An order placed sets the date to the order date, an order shipped sets it to the shipped date, an order due sets it to the required date. This is what earns the 'answers almost everything' claim.",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "Bridge grain = measurement event, with a per-event date column so every measure across every process lands on one canonical calendar; 'what happened in Q1' spans orders, shipments and returns on a single timeline without stitching star schemas."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The generator walks the business model as a graph. For each entity, it follows the relationships that go from many to one and inherits the keys and measures of everything it reaches. 'The rule is short: follow any path that never fans out, and inherit everything along it.'",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "Implement bridge construction as an n:1-only graph traversal with key/measure inheritance along each safe path (order line -> order -> customer means the order line inherits both)."
    },
    {
      "claim": "A many-to-one step is safe because many order lines rolling up to one order cannot double-count the order. A many-to-many step has no safe direction at all, so the generator skips it and tells you.",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "The generator must detect m:n edges, exclude them from the walk, and emit a warning naming the skipped edge \u2014 silent omission is not acceptable."
    },
    {
      "claim": "What you are left with, for every entity, is the exact set of dimensions its measures are additive along. 'Not additive if you remember to be careful. Additive by construction, with the safe paths worked out for you.'",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "Additivity is derived and guaranteed by the traversal, and must be materialized as an artifact, not left as tribal knowledge."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The model is self-describing: the same model that builds the bridge also knows, for every measure and every dimension, whether you can slice that measure by that dimension, and how. That is a sliceability contract.",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "Generation must emit a measure x dimension sliceability matrix alongside the bridge tables."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The sliceability contract 'doesn't have to live in a proprietary corner. Express it as a vendor-neutral semantic contract (e.g. OSI) and any BI tool or AI agent can read it directly.'",
      "layer": "cross-cutting",
      "implication": "Publish the contract in an open/vendor-neutral semantic format so BI tools and agents consume it directly; do not lock it into a vendor semantic layer."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Because the list of sliceable combinations is explicit, you can read off exactly which questions the model answers and which it does not, before you trust it with anything. 'Coverage is not a matter of faith.'",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "Coverage must be inspectable as a pre-trust artifact \u2014 the contract doubles as the coverage report."
